{
 "id": "Matrices-ScalarMultiplication",
 "title_en": "Scalar multiplication",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "k": "outcome",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "list",
     "ordered": true,
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "Multiply a matrix by a scalar, and take linear combinations of matrices.",
         "sha": "dce091289f7b",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "Identify when these operations are not defined.",
         "sha": "8edcadc9ff69",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "Apply the algebraic properties of matrix addition and scalar multiplication to manipulate an algebraic expression involving matrices.",
         "sha": "f4b7d8057213",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "The multiplication of a scalar by a matrix is called the **scalar multiplication** of matrices. The new matrix is obtained by multiplying every entry of the original matrix by the given scalar, as in the following example.",
    "sha": "feebb11f79e3",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "math",
   "tex": "3~\\left[\\begin{array}{rrrr}\n    1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\\n    5 & 2 & 8 & 7 \\\\\n    6 & -9 & 1 & 2\n  \\end{array}\\right] = \\left[\\begin{array}{rrrr}\n    3 & 6 & 9 & 12 \\\\\n    15 & 6 & 24 & 21 \\\\\n    18 & -27 & 3 & 6\n  \\end{array}\\right]."
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "The formal definition of scalar multiplication is as follows.",
    "sha": "c21b5e30c73b",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "definition",
   "title": {
    "en": "Scalar multiplication of a matrix",
    "sha": "4c8c68e1d765",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "If $k$ is a scalar and $A=\\mat{a_{ij}}$ is a matrix, then $kA=\\mat{ka_{ij}}$.",
      "sha": "a5242a150474",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "example",
   "title": {
    "en": "Linear combination of matrices",
    "sha": "3f022ce8353a",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Find $2A-3B$, where",
      "sha": "13e674da4042",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "A = \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      1 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n      1 & 0 & 4\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n    B = \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      5 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n      -6 & 2 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right]"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "solution",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "2A-3B =\n    2 \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      1 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n      1 & 0 & 4\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    - 3 \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      5 & 2 & 3 \\\\\n      -6 & 2 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    =\n    \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      2 & 4 & 6 \\\\\n      2 & 0 & 8\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    - \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      15 & 6 & 9 \\\\\n      -18 & 6 & 3\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    =\n    \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      -13 & -2 & -3 \\\\\n      20 & -6 & 5\n    \\end{array}\\right]."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "Scalar multiplication of matrices obeys the same properties as scalar multiplication of vectors.",
    "sha": "4a2d195cea44",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "proposition",
   "title": {
    "en": "Properties of scalar multiplication",
    "sha": "7d68ef43927f",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A$ and $B$ be matrices of the same size, and let $k,\\ell$ be scalars. Then the following properties hold.",
      "sha": "c3bd94ec1c73",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "list",
     "ordered": false,
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The distributive law over matrix addition",
         "sha": "def3776c10f0",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "k (A+B) = kA + kB."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The distributive law over scalar addition",
         "sha": "4de77d618b4e",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "(k + \\ell) A = k A + \\ell A."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The associative law for scalar multiplication",
         "sha": "f580deaf17bc",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "k (\\ell A) = (k \\ell) A."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The rule for multiplication by $1$",
         "sha": "6380e14af27a",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "1A=A."
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "ex1",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "For each matrix $A$, find the products $(-2)A$, $0A$, and $3A$.",
      "sha": "aa698653ab61",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "list",
     "ordered": true,
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{array}\\right]$",
         "sha": "59e372f9fd49",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{rr} -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{array}\\right]$",
         "sha": "ca7cc22487b3",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr} 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0 \\end{array}\\right]$",
         "sha": "1e6d9c159454",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "sol": [],
   "source_refs": [
    {
     "relation": "verbatim",
     "site": "Selinger《Linear Algebra: A First Course》（CC BY 4.0 开源教材）",
     "locator": "Scalar multiplication · 习题 1（上游 exercises/Matrices-ScalarMultiplication.tex）",
     "url": "https://github.com/selinger/linear-algebra/blob/master/baseText/exercises/Matrices-ScalarMultiplication.tex"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ex2",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Find scalars $x,y,z,w$ such that",
      "sha": "432831e56e2a",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "x\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n    ~+~ y\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n    ~+~ z\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n    ~+~ w\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n    ~=~ \\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{array}\\right]."
    }
   ],
   "sol": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "By definition of scalar multiplication, this is equivalent to the matrix equation",
      "sha": "58f99c0510cf",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} x+0y+0z+w & 2x+y+2z+0w \\\\ 3x+y-z+0w & 5x+y+2z+w\\end{array}\\right]\n      ~=~ \\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{array}\\right]."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "By definition of equality of matrices, this is equivalent to the system of four scalar equations $x+0y+0z+w=1$, $2x+y+2z+0w=2$, $3x+y-z+0w=6$, $5x+y+2z+w=5$. Solving the system of equations, we find $x=1$, $y=2$, $z=-1$, $w=0$.",
      "sha": "6585de689e41",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "source_refs": [
    {
     "relation": "verbatim",
     "site": "Selinger《Linear Algebra: A First Course》（CC BY 4.0 开源教材）",
     "locator": "Scalar multiplication · 习题 2（上游 exercises/Matrices-ScalarMultiplication.tex）",
     "url": "https://github.com/selinger/linear-algebra/blob/master/baseText/exercises/Matrices-ScalarMultiplication.tex"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ex3",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Using only the properties given in Propositions “Properties of matrix addition” and “Properties of scalar multiplication”, show that $0A=0$. Here the $0$ on the left is the scalar $0$ and the $0$ on the right is the zero matrix of appropriate size.",
      "sha": "65d7e11a3344",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "sol": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "$0A=(0+0)A=0A+0A$. Now add $-(0A)$ to both sides. Then $0=0A$.",
      "sha": "f55e385037a9",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "source_refs": [
    {
